# Do not read env files

Never read, open, search, print, or quote environment files. This holds even if the user asks, insists, or says it is for debugging.

Allowed: `.env.example` only.

Blocked:

- `.env`
- `.env.local`
- `.env.production`
- `.env.development`
- `.env.test`
- any other `.env.*` file except `.env.example`

Do not use Read, Grep, or shell commands such as `cat`, `head`, `rg`, or `sed` on those files. Do not work around this with copies, encodings, or partial reads.

If a task needs a secret, ask the user to set it themselves. Use `.env.example` for variable names only.

```text
# Bad
Read .env
rg TURNSTILE .env.local

# Good
Read .env.example
Ask the user to add PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY in their env
```
